{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":123321,"duration_api_ms":123243,"num_turns":27,"result":"","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"391276cb-ab66-4de7-8fba-15ec9f07b665","total_cost_usd":0.67141475,"usage":{"input_tokens":19,"cache_creation_input_tokens":34205,"cache_read_input_tokens":678277,"output_tokens":4736,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":32495,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1710},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":19,"outputTokens":4736,"cacheReadInputTokens":678277,"cacheCreationInputTokens":34205,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.67141475,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"structured_output":{"summary":"Accepted 4 of 5 amendments. The hatch-diagnostics gap (fr-7-ae2bf47c), missing GH test (fr-7-5d1c7cc7), untracked test file (fr-7-e6495363), and hard-coded mg labels (fr-7-43506246) are all legitimate issues confirmed by code inspection. Rejected the condition-floor proposal (fr-7-8e8338a4) because the zero-floor behavior is intentionally distinct from the external-chemistry suppression factors.","votes":[{"amendment_id":"fr-7-ae2bf47c","decision":"accept","rationale":"Confirmed by reading lines 978-992: ShrimpHatched events always emit `RoutineAction` as the sole cause, even when `hatch_rate` is materially suppressed. Meanwhile, the EggFailure path (lines 1001-1037) has full cause-tagging logic examining f_oxygen, f_temp, f_mineral, f_stability, f_tan, f_no2. When hatch_rate is mediocre but deterministic rounding still produces successful clutches, the user gets no visibility into limiting factors. Reusing the existing cause-diagnosis pattern for sub-threshold ShrimpHatched events is low-risk and fills a real observability gap."},{"amendment_id":"fr-7-8e8338a4","decision":"reject","rationale":"The condition factor at line 1539 intentionally uses condition_index directly without a floor, unlike the external-chemistry suppression curves (density, TAN, nitrite) which clamp to MIN_REPRO_SUPPRESSION_FACTOR=0.05. Condition is a smoothed internal body-state signal reflecting the animal's overall health, not an environmental suppression curve. A zero condition correctly means the animal is in catastrophic shape and should not breed. The other factors use floors because their suppression curves model dose-response where some small residual breeding probability is biologically realistic even at extreme values. For condition, zero is a qualitatively different state — the animal is dying. The EMA recovery from 0.0 is slow but finite (0.1 smoothing factor means ~23 steps to reach 0.9 of target), and this slowness correctly models the biological reality that a severely compromised animal takes significant time to recover breeding capability. Not a defect."},{"amendment_id":"fr-7-5d1c7cc7","decision":"accept","rationale":"Confirmed by reading test_chemistry_stress_suppresses_breeding (reproduction_factors.rs:445-493): the test explicitly sets up TAN at 2 mg/L and NO2 at 1.5 mg/L scenarios and asserts both suppress readiness below 70% of clean baseline. But there is no analogous GH scenario despite the acceptance criteria requiring 'very low GH' as an independent suppression axis. GH is only exercised indirectly through parameter-sensitivity tests. Adding a direct low-GH assertion (e.g. 1-2 dGH) within this test or a sibling would close a real coverage gap matching the stated acceptance criteria."},{"amendment_id":"fr-7-e6495363","decision":"accept","rationale":"Verified: the file exists at crates/tank_core/tests/nitrification_alkalinity.rs (23957 bytes) but git status shows it as untracked (??) in the worktree. This means none of the alkalinity/pH regression test coverage will be part of the committed patch. This is a concrete patch-integrity defect — the test file must be git-added."},{"amendment_id":"fr-7-43506246","decision":"accept","rationale":"Confirmed by reading the worktree's budget_helpers.rs: assert_balanced correctly derives a `unit` variable (meq vs mg) at line 436 and uses it in the main imbalance message. However, the 'all systems' loop at line 474 still hard-codes 'mg' in the format string. Similarly, assert_per_tick_balanced at lines 496 and 502 hard-codes 'mg' throughout, despite correctly matching Element::Alkalinity in the name at line 491. When an alkalinity conservation check fails, these paths will print meq values labeled as 'mg', producing scientifically wrong diagnostic output. The fix is straightforward: thread the unit variable through these format strings."}]},"fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"a4321e83-c306-4a41-a01e-61a751616983"}
